  "illustration_prompt": "Create a single publication-quality landscape 3:2 narrative illustration for an adult illustrated English-reading booklet. Finished standalone artwork, not a book mockup, not a collage of multiple unrelated panels. Sophisticated contemporary editorial gouache and watercolor, carefully controlled colored-pencil accents, velvety matte paint, subtle warm paper texture, soft natural daylight. Warm ivory paper dominates; subdued eucalyptus and sage greens structure the composition; restrained buttery lemon-yellow and dusty terracotta accents. Calm, comfortable, refined, credible drawing and natural anatomy. An airy coherent composition with one clear story focal point and at most two supporting details, generous breathing room at all edges and softly feathered painted edges into ivory. Detailed but uncluttered. Historically and scientifically appropriate objects, clothing and setting. Show the particular article's pivotal event or mechanism rather than a generic portrait or generic textbook symbols. Human figures treated with dignity; no grotesque caricature, no sensational violence or visible injuries. For disasters, emphasize the setting, evidence or human response without graphic suffering. For uncertain scientific claims, depict the observations and investigation without presenting debated mechanisms as proven. Absolutely no typography, captions, letters, numbers, logos, watermarks, diagram labels, fake writing or invented charts anywhere. Do not render invisible forces as literal magic. Avoid corporate vector icons, slick 3D rendering and childish cartoon style. High-quality, print-ready visual storytelling, matching a warm ivory-and-sage adult watercolor booklet.\n\nARTICLE63: Quiet editorial watercolor of the aftermath of an accidental satellite collision above Earth. One damaged, unmarked satellite body with a broken solar-panel edge and a few separated small metal fragments is the main focal object. A distant intact unmarked satellite and a gentle curve of Earth are the only two supporting elements. Show only a sparse local group of fragments, not an encircling Saturn-like debris ring and not a total catastrophic cascade. The distant operational satellite remains undamaged, suggesting the continuing need to avoid fragments rather than an inevitable chain reaction. Muted slate-sage metal, terracotta-grey panel detail, pale blue-grey Earth blending into warm ivory paper; no literal flames in vacuum, laser paths, written satellite names, orbit arrows, charts or labels. Credible spacecraft structure, restrained and contemplative, not glossy science-fiction 3D.",
